{"name":"Kuretake Black Manga","hex":"#001122","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"black","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Absorbing all light and reflecting none; dark and hueless.","Without light."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour/color perceived in the absence of light, but also when no light is reflected, but rather absorbed.","A black dye or pigment."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/black"},{"term":"manga","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A comic originating in Japan.","An artistic style heavily used in, and associated with, Japanese comics, and that has also been adopted by a comparatively low number of comics from other countries."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A covering for a crucifix."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/manga"}],"entries":[{"title":"Kuretake (art products)","blocks":[{"text":"Kuretake Co., Ltd. is a Japanese manufacturing company of writing implements. The firm began its activities manufacturing sumi ink and brushes and then expanded its range of products, producing mainly pens."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kuretake_(art_products)","wikidata":"Q6445898"},{"title":"Manga","blocks":[{"text":"Manga  are comics or graphic novels originating from Japan. Most manga conform to a style developed in Japan in the late 19th century, and the form has a long history in earlier Japanese art. The term manga is used in Japan to refer to both comics and cartooning. Outside of Japan, the word is typically used to refer to comics originally published in Japan."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Wikipe-tan_manga_page1.jpg/330px-Wikipe-tan_manga_page1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manga","wikidata":"Q8274"},{"title":"Black","blocks":[{"text":"Black is a color that results from the absence or complete absorption of visible light. It is an achromatic color, without chroma, like white and grey. It is often used symbolically or figuratively to represent darkness. Black and white have often been used to describe opposites such as good and evil, the Dark Ages versus the Age of Enlightenment, and night versus day. Since the Middle Ages, black has been the symbolic color of solemnity and authority, and for this reason it is still commonly worn by judges and magistrates."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Tutankhamun_jackal_%28blacked%29.png/330px-Tutankhamun_jackal_%28blacked%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black","wikidata":"Q23445"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/b/b4/Sazae-san_kamishibai.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/b/b4/Sazae-san_kamishibai.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"A kamishibai storyteller from Sazae-san by Machiko Hasegawa. Sazae appears with her hair in a bun.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASazae-san_kamishibai.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Manga_Hokusai.jpg/330px-Manga_Hokusai.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Manga_Hokusai.jpg/330px-Manga_Hokusai.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hokusai Manga depicting self-defense techniques (early 19th century)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AManga_Hokusai.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Delegates_of_3rd_Asian_Cartoon_Exhibition.JPG/500px-Delegates_of_3rd_Asian_Cartoon_Exhibition.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikime